{
  "$schema": "./node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "predict-safe",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-10",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "services": [
    {
      "binding": "WORKER_SELF_REFERENCE",
      "service": "predict-safe"
    }
  ],
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },

  /*
   * Runtime environment variables.
   *
   * `wrangler deploy` treats this file as the source of truth for `vars`:
   * anything configured in the Cloudflare dashboard but *not* listed here is
   * removed from the Worker on the next CLI deploy. That is not a hypothetical
   * - it is what took the site's football data offline on 19 Sep 2026. A
   * deploy ran from a checkout whose config declared no `vars` at all, which
   * cleared API_FOOTBALL_KEY in production, and every fixtures, odds, live
   * score and free-pick request started failing with "Missing required
   * environment variable".
   *
   * So: anything production needs at runtime belongs either here or in the
   * Worker's secrets. Nowhere else.
   *
   * Only values that are safe to commit live in this file. Every NEXT_PUBLIC_*
   * value is compiled into the browser bundle and served to every visitor, so
   * there is nothing to protect by hiding it here. They are still listed
   * because server-side code (lib/supabase/server.ts, proxy.ts) reads them
   * from process.env at request time, not just at build time.
   */
  "vars": {
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID": "G-7KDF121W18",
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL": "https://www.predictsafe.com",
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZSIsInJlZiI6InV0cmRkY3lueWpxdWRmYmVqdmZ4Iiwicm9sZSI6ImFub24iLCJpYXQiOjE3OTA4MDgzMjIsImV4cCI6MjEwNjM4NDMyMn0.bnesF8ExUK3Dm6i2nyh8vTDt1lkn2ZzLWWNRzlzNQK4",
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL": "https://utrddcynyjqudfbejvfx.supabase.co",
    "SMTP_USER": "predictsafe@gmail.com"
  }

  /*
   * SECRETS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS FILE.
   *
   * This repository is PUBLIC. This file is tracked in it. Anything added to
   * the `vars` block above is readable by anyone on the internet, immediately
   * and permanently - rotating the credential is the only way back, because
   * every clone and fork keeps a copy of the history.
   *
   * That is not theoretical here: a live API-Sports key reached the public
   * repo through .claude/settings.json, where permission entries had recorded
   * curl commands with the key inline in a header. It had to be rotated.
   *
   * SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY in particular must never be committed. It
   * bypasses row-level security entirely, so it is not "the database key" so
   * much as unrestricted read, write and delete over every user, subscription
   * and transaction in the project.
   *
   * Cloudflare stores secrets separately from `vars`, encrypted, and a deploy
   * does NOT clear them - which is also why a secret is the *correct* fix for
   * values that keep disappearing on deploy, not merely the safer one. Putting
   * them in `vars` is what made them fragile in the first place.
   *
   * Production needs these set as secrets:
   *
   *   npx wrangler secret put API_FOOTBALL_KEY   # API-Sports v3 key
   *   npx wrangler secret put SMTP_PASSWORD      # Gmail app password
   *   npx wrangler secret put SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
   *
   * SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is what lets generated free picks be written to
   * the generated_free_picks table. Without it the picks still work and are
   * still deterministic, but they are rebuilt per request instead of stored,
   * so the guarantee that every visitor sees an identical set is weaker.
   *
   * List what is currently set with:  npx wrangler secret list
   *
   * API_FOOTBALL_BASE_URL is not configured anywhere; lib/api-football.ts
   * defaults to https://v3.football.api-sports.io, which is correct.
   */
}
